<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cheka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as All-Russian Extraordinary Commission</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cheka, the All-Russian Extraordinary Commission, was the first Soviet secret police, founded in December 1917 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-dzerzhinsky">Felix Dzerzhinsky</a> to fight counter-revolution and sabotage. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> it carried out the Red Terror, with mass arrests, hostage-taking and executions without trial. In 1922 it was replaced by the GPU, a forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> and the KGB.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cheka.jpg" alt="Cheka" width="176" height="285" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GPU_5th_anniversary_emblem.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the first weeks after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> the task of suppressing resistance fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd-military-revolutionary-committee">Petrograd Military Revolutionary Committee</a>, a temporary body. On 6 December 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a> decided that a special commission was needed to apply the most energetically revolutionary measures. It was established on 20 December 1917 (7 December Old Style), with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-dzerzhinsky">Felix Dzerzhinsky</a> as its head. Its official name was the All-Russian Extraordinary Commission for Combating Counter-Revolution and Sabotage, shortened to VChK or Cheka, and its members were called Chekists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In its first months the Cheka had only 40 officials, with a regiment of soldiers and a group of Red Guards, and worked mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>. It soon had sections for railways and branches in every guberniya and uyezd. By the end of the Civil War it had at least 200,000 personnel. Although it was created to protect the revolution from class enemies such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> and the clergy, within a month it had extended its repression to all political opponents of the Bolshevik government, including anarchists. On 11 and 12 April 1918 it attacked 26 anarchist centres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, killing forty anarchists and arresting about 500.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>'s direction it carried out mass arrests, imprisonments and executions without trial in what came to be known as the Red Terror, which Dzerzhinsky implemented on 5 September 1918. Its special punitive troops fought desertion from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, which had more than three million deserters in 1919 and 1920, and took and shot hostages to force compliance. It also policed labour camps, requisitioned food and put down worker and peasant rebellions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Estimates of its executions vary widely. Its own officer Martyn Latsis gave 12,733 for 1918 to 1920, a figure that historians generally regard as far too low. Estimates run from at least 50,000 to as many as 200,000, and many put the total at about 100,000. The Cheka was dissolved in February 1922 and succeeded by the State Political Directorate, the GPU. It was the first of a long succession of Soviet secret police bodies, and the name Chekists long remained a name for their members.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cheka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the Soviet Armenian historian Bagrat Borian, indiscriminate seizures and confiscations; requisitioning and the terror of the local <b>Cheka</b> led the Armenians, under former leaders of the First Republic, to revolt in February 1921 and briefly unseat the government in Yerevan, until the Red Army returned from Georgia…</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In practice power lay with the Communist Party, whose Politburo decided policy, and with the secret police, the <b>Cheka</b>, founded in December 1917 under Felix Dzerzhinsky, which waged a Red Terror against opponents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The political police, successively the <b>Cheka</b>, OGPU, NKVD and from 1954 the KGB, enforced conformity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The secret police, the <b>Cheka</b> and its successor the GPU, acted as a &quot;state within a state&quot;, staging annual show trials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Civil War the remaining parties were alternately tolerated and repressed by the <b>Cheka</b>, the political police.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…land of the church and nobility to the peasants but took their surplus grain, shut down opposing newspapers and founded the <b>Cheka</b> as a secret police; in January 1918 they dissolved the Constituent Assembly, in which they had won 23.3% of the vote to the Socialist Revolutionaries’ 37.6%.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
